760 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 171 0 R
/T (An operational structure is the way a business organizes its processes, people, and resources to achieve its daily goals. It specifies how various roles and tasks are divided, coordinated, and monitored to ensure a smooth workflow, clear responsibilities, and effective decision-making \(Daft, 2021\). Daft \(2021\) explains how a well-designed operational structure enables businesses to increase efficiency, manage growth, and provide consistent customer service. There are several types of operational structures, including flat structures, which have no hierarchy and promote direct communication, and functional structures, which group employees based on specific tasks or skills, such as administration, technical work, or customer service.  )
/E (An operational structure is the way a business organizes its processes, people, and resources to achieve its daily goals. It specifies how various roles and tasks are divided, coordinated, and monitored to ensure a smooth workflow, clear responsibilities, and effective decision-making \(Daft, 2021\). Daft \(2021\) explains how a well-designed operational structure enables businesses to increase efficiency, manage growth, and provide consistent customer service. There are several types of operational structures, including flat structures, which have no hierarchy and promote direct communication, and functional structures, which group employees based on specific tasks or skills, such as administration, technical work, or customer service.  )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
761 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 171 0 R
/T (According to Daft \(2021\), a functional structure is an organizational model in which employees are classified based on their specific roles, skills, or job functions within a company. This structure clearly defines responsibilities and reporting lines, which improves performance and allows employees to specialize in their specific tasks. Operations, administration, customer service, and technical services are some examples of typical business departments or functions. The functional structure is especially useful for businesses that need specialised expertise while maintaining control and clear communication within each area.  )
